🟢 Phase 8 — Kubernetes ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐[4weeks]
FIRST UNDERSTAND        API = application programming interface
-----------------------
Cluster = A Kubernetes cluster is a group of computers (machines) working together to run applications
Node    = one machine inside that cluster
Control Plane = The Control Plane is the brain of a Kubernetes cluster. 🧠
Worker Node   = A worker node normally has three important things:
           Worker Node
│
├── Kubelet = Communicates with the Control Plane and makes sure the required Pods are running.
├── Container Runtime = Actually runs the containers. A common runtime is containerd.
└── Pods  = Your applications run inside Pods.
Pod   = group of container

Container 📦
│
├── Application
├── Libraries
├── Dependencies
└── Configuration
Deployment = 
A Deployment is a Kubernetes object that manages Pods for you.
It tells Kubernetes things like:
----------------------------------
Which container image to use
How many Pods you want
Which version of the application to run
What to do when a Pod crashes
How to update the application

-deployment also work rolling update

ReplicaSet = A ReplicaSet (RS) is a Kubernetes object that makes sure a specific number of identical Pods are always running
           = it is not update rollout 
Service  = A Service in Kubernetes provides a stable way to access Pods.
         Pod = your application
         Service = the fixed address used to reach the application
Why do we need a Service?
Pods are temporary. A Pod can be deleted and recreated, and its IP address can change.

Namespace = A Namespace is like a separate room/folder inside a Kubernetes cluster used to organize and separate resources
simple example 
Kubernetes Cluster
│
├── Development Namespace
│   ├── Pods
│   ├── Services
│   └── Deployments
│
├── Testing Namespace
│   ├── Pods
│   ├── ServicesVery 
│   └── Deployments
│
└── Production Namespace
    ├── Pods
    ├── Services
    └── Deployments
ConfigMap =  A ConfigMap is used to store configuration data 
from your application/container.
Very simple example
APP_NAME = Netflix
APP_PORT = 8080
ENVIRONMENT = production

Secret = A Secret in Kubernetes is used to store sensitive information that your application needs.
ConfigMap = Normal information
Secret    = Sensitive information

Ingress = Ingress is a Kubernetes object that controls how outside users access applications inside the cluster, usually using HTTP/HTTPS.
Ingress = traffic entry point + routing rules

Volume  = A Volume in Kubernetes is a way to provide storage to a Pod/container
Container = temporary workspace
Volume = storage that can keep data available

